"""Specific study guidance, kept separate from generated card counts."""
NOTES = {
 'presente': ('Practising the presente', 'Use these prompts to recall present indicative forms across different persons. Italian often leaves out the subject pronoun, so pay attention to the ending as well as the English subject. These drills practise forms; the present can describe habits, general facts and some actions in progress, depending on context.'),
 'passato_prossimo': ('Practising the passato prossimo', 'Recall the complete compound form, including avere or essere and the past participle. The English prompt may use a simple past or “have” form. When studying the grammar alongside the cards, check auxiliary choice and participle agreement rather than treating the participle as an isolated word.'),
 'imperfetto': ('Imperfetto and passato prossimo', 'The imperfetto often describes past habits, background situations or actions viewed as ongoing. English prompts such as “used to” and “was doing” are practice cues, not a rule that covers every sentence. Compare the context with the passato prossimo when reading Italian; a conjugation card alone cannot make that choice for you.'),
 'futuro_semplice': ('Practising the futuro semplice', 'Focus on the future stem and person ending as a complete form: for example, vedrai means “you will see”. Regular endings help, but some verbs have irregular stems. Italian also uses the present for some future plans, so these prompts are conjugation practice rather than a requirement to use the future in every future context.'),
 'presente_progressivo': ('Stare with the gerund', 'The progressive uses a form of stare followed by a gerund, as in stiamo vedendo. Recall both parts of the answer. This deck focuses on the present progressive; it is separate from the presente deck. Do not assume every English “-ing” phrase needs a progressive construction in Italian.'),
 'condizionale_presente': ('Practising the condizionale presente', 'These cards practise forms such as vedrebbe, “he or she would see”. Study the conditional stem and ending together. In context, the conditional can appear in polite requests, wishes and hypothetical statements. If-sentences have their own grammar; a “would” prompt here is a cue to recall a form, not a complete explanation of those constructions.'),
 'condizionale_passato': ('Practising the condizionale passato', 'Recall the conditional form of avere or essere together with the past participle: avremmo visto means “we would have seen”. Check the auxiliary and any agreement required by the verb. This is a compound conditional deck, distinct from the single-word forms in condizionale presente.'),
 'imperativo_con_tu': ('The imperative with tu', 'These prompts address one person informally. Practise the imperative form rather than automatically using the second-person present indicative. Negative commands with tu use a different pattern, so keep the polarity of the prompt in mind. Pronouns and irregular commands also deserve attention when you meet them in context.'),
 'imperativo_con_lei': ('The formal imperative with Lei', 'These prompts address one person formally. The forms follow the present subjunctive pattern used for formal commands; they differ from the informal tu imperative. Keep the formal address in mind even when the English prompt does not show a clear difference. Study pronoun placement alongside the form.'),
 'imperativo_con_noi': ('The imperative with noi', 'These prompts suggest an action that includes the speaker, as in “let’s think”. The forms overlap with first-person plural present forms, but their function in the prompt is a suggestion or invitation. Read the English cue before recalling the Italian instead of identifying the use from the ending alone.'),
 'imperativo_con_voi': ('The imperative with voi', 'These prompts address more than one person. Focus on the plural command and distinguish it from the informal singular tu form. The forms often overlap with the second-person plural present indicative, so sentence context still matters when you encounter them outside the deck.'),
}


def notes_for(stem):
    for prefix in ('italian_verbs_learning_', 'italian_verbs_more_tenses_'):
        if stem.startswith(prefix):
            return NOTES.get(stem.removeprefix(prefix))
    return {
        'italian_basics_numbers': ('Working on easily confused numbers', 'The deck gives particular attention to teens and tens, including forms such as undici. Try recalling a number in both directions and saying it aloud. A written answer can feel easier than recognising a number in speech; use the recording and practise in contexts such as prices or times alongside the cards.'),
        'italian_core_fluent_forever_625': ('A core-vocabulary starting point', 'This deck groups vocabulary around everyday subjects rather than a CILS level. The source-list name is not a claim that the current package contains exactly 625 notes. Use the current counts above and inspect the sample. It shares everyday words with the CILS decks, so importing both can repeat material.'),
        'italian_frequency': ('What a frequency list can tell you', 'The source ranks verbs and nouns found in film and television dialogue. This is not a complete list of every Italian word or a promise that every entry will be equally useful to you. Dialogue frequency can differ from the vocabulary you need for a textbook, a workplace or a particular conversation.'),
        'italian_noun_phrases': ('Recall the whole phrase', 'Pay attention to the article, possessive or preposition as well as the noun. A prompt such as “their world” asks for il loro mondo, including the article. These drills complement vocabulary study: recognising mondo on its own is a different task from producing the full phrase.'),
        'italian_cognates': ('Similar words still need context', 'Connections between English and Italian can make a word easier to recall, but similar spelling does not guarantee the same meaning in every context. Use the prompt and any sense note on the card. Keep checking unfamiliar uses when reading rather than treating a cognate as an automatic word-for-word substitution.'),
        'italian_movies_le_otto_montagne': ('Using the film vocabulary', 'This deck draws on the Italian subtitles of Le otto montagne (2022). The words come from that specific source, so some will be less general than a beginner vocabulary list. Use the quoted line to connect the meaning to its context. The literal English gloss is a study aid and may not read like a natural subtitle.'),
        'italian_movies_benvenuti_al_sud': ('Using dialogue as context', 'This deck draws on the Italian subtitles of Benvenuti al Sud (2010). Subtitle vocabulary reflects this film’s dialogue and setting; it is not a graded course. The example line and literal English gloss help locate a word in context, while watching and listening provide information that an isolated flashcard cannot.'),
    }.get(stem)
